# Errors and logging

## User-facing errors

- One `AppError` enum crosses layer boundaries. Repositories translate transport, decoding, and persistence failures into it.
- Cases stay payload-free (apart from data the UI shows, like an HTTP status). What the UI needs — title, message, whether retry is offered — hangs off the enum as computed properties, so the copy lives in one place instead of at every throw site.
- Never surface a raw `Error`, a `URLError.localizedDescription`, or a decoding error to a user.
- Failure is a state, not a side effect: deliver it through the view model's state enum, not a stray alert flag.

```swift
enum AppError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {
    case offline
    case timedOut
    case server(status: Int)
    case decoding
    case persistence
    case unknown

    var title: String { ... }
    var message: String { ... }
    var isRetryable: Bool { ... }
}
```

**`AppError` never carries the underlying `Error`.** A `case underlying(any Error)` would break the synthesized `Equatable` that state assertions depend on. Log the root cause at the translation site in the repository, then erase it:

```swift
catch let error as DecodingError {
    logger.error("Top movies decode failed: \(error)", category: .networking)
    throw AppError.decoding
}
```

That keeps the user-facing error comparable and the real cause in the log.

## Logging

- Log through an injected abstraction. Never `print`.

```swift
protocol AppLogging: Sendable {
    func debug(_ message: String, category: LogCategory)
    func error(_ message: String, category: LogCategory)
}
```

- The production implementation wraps `os.Logger` with subsystem `com.urbn.URBNFlicks` and a fixed `LogCategory` set: `networking`, `persistence`, `ui`, `navigation`.
- Log failures and lifecycle transitions. Never log API keys, tokens, or full response bodies.
